<p>An <code>async</code> function <em>always</em> returns a <code>Promise</code> and the <code>Promise</code> it returns will resolve to the returned value or reject with an <code>Error</code> if an <code>Error</code> is thrown, so you typically don't need to use a <code>try / catch</code> in the <code>async</code> function body.</p>

